; Minimal probe for stringSplit (contract entry 39): one node, four receivers.
;
; **No fixture here contains an empty token, and that is the point of this
; note.** Contract §2.39 (A4) adjudicates `split` to skip empty tokens, which is
; what the LLVM arm's `strtok` loop already does; the interpreter still keeps
; them, because `StdlibPini.split` appends its accumulator unconditionally. The
; interpreter-side alignment is the `stringSplit` grid's job and is registered
; there, so the two channels disagree about `"a,,b"` today.
;
; This harness asserts the channels agree. A fixture holding an empty token would
; therefore fail -- not because this node is wrong, but because the disagreement
; is real and filed elsewhere. So the empty-token case cannot live in the corpus
; until that grid lands, and its absence is a known blind spot rather than
; coverage. Consequence worth stating plainly: the corpus cannot tell a correct
; `split` from one with the opposite empty-token rule.
;
; What is covered: a multi-part split, a single-part split (no delimiter
; present), the element count, and a delimiter that does appear but only once.
; The array shape is as much a part of this node as the text is.

main|func() -> ():
    var s = "Hello, World"
    var parts = s.split(",")
    print(parts)
    print(len(parts))
    var more = "a-b-c".split("-")
    print(more)
    print(len(more))
    var none = "abc".split("-")
    print(none)
    print(len(none))
    var once = "a-b".split("-")
    print(once)
    print(len(once))
    return
